['**Question:**
\begin{equation}
\textbf{If } R_{1} \text{ and } R_{2} \text{ are equivalence relations in set A}, \text{ show that } R_{1} \cap R_{2} \text{ is also an equivalence relation.}
\end{equation}

**Answer:**
\begin{equation}
\textbf{Since } R_{1} \text{ and } R_{2} \text{ are equivalence relations, } (a,a) \in R_{1} \text{ and } (a,a) \in R_{2} \text{ for all } a \in A. \\
\text{This implies that } (a,a) \in R_{2}, \text{ showing } R_{1} \cap R_{2} \text{ is reflexive. Further, if } (a,b) \in R_{1} \cap R_{2}, \text{ then } (a,b) \in R_{1} \text{ and } (a,b) \in R_{2} \text{ hence, } R_{1} \cap R_{2} \text{ is symmetric. Similarly, if } (a,b) \in R_{1} \cap R_{2}, \text{ then } (a,c) \in R_{1} \cap R_{2}. \\
\textbf{Thus, } R_{1} \cap R_{2} \text{ is an equivalence relation.}
