orig_q:
Solve the following pair of equations by substitution method:
\begin{align*}
7x - 15y &= 2 \quad (1) \\
x + 2y &= 3 \quad (2)
\end{align*}

pert_a:
\text{Step 1:} We pick either of the equations and write one variable in terms of the other. Let us consider Equation (2):
\begin{align*}
x + 2y &= 3 \\
x &= 3 - 2y \quad (3)
\end{align*}

\text{Step 2:} Substitute the value of \(x\) in Equation (1):
\begin{align*}
7(3 - 2y) - 15y &= 2 \\
21 - 14y - 15y &= 2 \\
21 - 29y &= 2 \\
-29y &= -19 \\
y &= \frac{19}{29}
\end{align*}

\text{Step 3:} Substituting this value of \(y\) in Equation (3), we get:
\begin{align*}
x &= 3 - 2\left(\frac{19}{29}\right) = \frac{49}{29}
\end{align*}

\textcolor{red}{\text{Therefore, the value of \(x\) is } x = \frac{49}{29}.}

 \textcolor{red}{Option B}

\textcolor{red}{Angle traced by minute hand in 60 minutes = 360°.}

\textcolor{red}{Angle traced by minute hand in 10 minutes = $\left( \frac{360}{60} \times 10 \right)° = 60°$.}

\textcolor{red}{So the minute hand has moved through 60° by 10 minutes past 5.}

